winewayland: give each tablet tool the window's Win32 cursor (zwp_tablet_tool_v2.set_cursor / cursor-shape get_tablet_tool_v2)

What a user sees (Neutron#56, Photoshop 2025, runtime 11.10-213, AMD, KDE Plasma 6.8.2): "When pen tablet are used the cursor
suddenly changed to cross shaped made it hard to use for drawing purposes. While using mouse on the other hand the cursor
follow brush shape as it should be." The owner sees the same crosshair with his Cintiq.

Mechanism: the Win32 side was already right. wintab.c injects the pen's mouse input with NtUserSendHardwareInput(hwnd), the
window answers WM_SETCURSOR, and win32u hands the HCURSOR to WAYLAND_SetCursor -- which gave it to wl_pointer only, and only
while the POINTER was over that window. On Wayland every tablet tool has its own cursor; winewayland binds the tablet seat in
every process but never called zwp_tablet_tool_v2.set_cursor or cursor-shape-v1's get_tablet_tool_v2, so KWin drew its
fallback crosshair for the pen over every Wine window (KWin's fallback for a client that never sets a tool cursor: KDE bug
477570, the same bug Qt had before 6.8).

Windows: the tablet driver moves the one system cursor; the pen shows exactly what the mouse shows (Photoshop's brush outline,
its Caps Lock precise cross, arrows over panels).
Fix: each tool (pen, eraser: its own wl_surface, as a surface may only ever be one tool's cursor, and its own shape device)
shows the Win32 cursor of the window it is in proximity over, resolved by the pointer's own code (cursor-shape for system
cursors, else the bitmap with hotspot and viewport at the window's scale, the retained last cursor for an unresolvable
handle, hidden for NULL / ShowCursor(FALSE)): at every proximity_in (the cursor is undefined there, as at wl_pointer.enter),
at every WAYLAND_SetCursor for that window, and over another process's child window as pointer_motion_to_hwnd does. Every
request carries the tool's latest proximity_in serial. The pointer's buffer/surface helpers now take the cursor to fill;
the pointer path is unchanged (its request stream is identical with and without this patch, and on 214).

Not covered: over a child window of ANOTHER process the pen, like the pointer, shows the shape last published to the shared
slot / the last resolved cursor, not that process's own cursor (lab: a SIZEALL child showed the parent's last shape, same for
the pointer) -- pre-existing pointer behavior. Pads (zwp_tablet_pad_v2) have no cursor. Not proven on hardware in the lab: KWin
drawing the tool cursor (protocol-standard; the owner's hand test decides).

Lab evidence (no tablet in a nested desk, no uinput: a Wayland wire proxy in front of the nested KWin 6.8.3 injected a fake
tablet + pen + eraser into Wine's connection and logged every request Wine sent on them; probe curprobe.exe cycles arrow /
64 px color ring / hand / SetCursor(NULL) / 32 px mono X / I-beam / ShowCursor(FALSE), 175% output, 168 dpi):
  fix (11.10-214pc) 5/5 runs: pen and eraser get exactly the probe's cursor in every phase (shape 1/4/9, ring 64x64 hotspot
  18,18 dest 37x37, X 32x32 hotspot 9,9 dest 18x18, hidden), each with its own latest proximity serial (5001/5002/5003), and the
  same requests and byte-identical cursor images as wl_pointer for the same phases (4/4 runs with a held fake pointer);
  kill switch 3/3 and 214 4/4: zero tool requests (= the crosshair); wl_pointer's requests identical in all arms.
  Photoshop 2026 lab copy (UseSystemStylus 0, WinTab), fake pen, 2 fix runs + 1 kill run: brush tool hover -> the pen gets
  Photoshop's brush outline bitmap (13x13, hotspot centered), ] x3 -> 21x21 centered, Caps Lock on -> Photoshop's precise
  cursor (64x64), off -> outline again, over the right-hand panels -> arrow (shape 1), back on the canvas -> outline, eraser
  end with the Eraser tool -> 15x15 outline; eyedropper run: the eyedropper bitmap, its precise cursor on Caps Lock;
  kill switch: zero tool requests (pointer requests unchanged).
Kill switch: NEUTRON_TABLET_CURSOR=0. Diag: NEUTRON_TABLET_CURSOR_DIAG=1 (err+waylanddrv: one neutron-tabletcursor line per
tool cursor update).
11.10-213.1 (hotfix line): regenerated with diff -u on 11.10-213's tree; hunk bodies identical to main's 227a38, offsets
only (waylanddrv.h -5/-66, wayland_pointer.c -24, wayland_tablet.c 0).

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
@@ -210,6 +210,17 @@
                                    * channel can be motion-gated like the real Wacom driver
                                    * (which emits nothing when the pen hasn't moved) */
     BOOL  pending_move;
+    /* NEUTRON tablet cursor (NEUTRON_TABLET_CURSOR): the tool's own cursor. On Windows the pen
+     * drives the one system cursor; on Wayland every tablet tool has its own, set with
+     * zwp_tablet_tool_v2.set_cursor (or cursor-shape-v1's get_tablet_tool_v2) against the
+     * tool's proximity_in serial. A wl_surface may only ever be the cursor of ONE tool, so each
+     * tool owns its surface. Guarded by process_wayland.pointer.mutex. */
+    HWND cursor_hwnd;             /* window the tool is in proximity over, NULL when out */
+    uint32_t proximity_serial;    /* serial of the latest proximity_in */
+    struct wayland_cursor cursor;
+    struct wp_cursor_shape_device_v1 *wp_cursor_shape_device_v1;
+    BOOL cursor_xproc;            /* last motion was over another process's child window */
+    UINT cursor_xproc_shape;      /* the shape shown there (0 = hidden) */
 };
 
